WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.269
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.222 --> 00:00:07.930
CARTER MORGAN: Welcome
to another episode

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:10.930
of "VM End-to-End," a show
where we have a VM skeptic

00:00:10.930 --> 00:00:12.550
and a VM enthusiast
come together

00:00:12.550 --> 00:00:14.710
to hash out all
things VM related.

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:16.930
Thanks for being
here today, Brian.

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:18.280
BRIAN DORSEY: Happy to be here.

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:19.540
What's the topic for today?

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:20.770
CARTER MORGAN: Oh,
you know what it is.

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:22.093
I need to know about identity.

00:00:22.093 --> 00:00:23.260
I need to know about access.

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:25.690
I want to know why I can't
just SSH into everything

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:26.830
or if I can.

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:28.462
What do you got for me, Brian?

00:00:28.462 --> 00:00:29.170
BRIAN DORSEY: OK.

00:00:29.170 --> 00:00:32.740
So a little quickie
bit here, and then I'm

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:34.265
going to bring a
guest in because I

00:00:34.265 --> 00:00:35.390
don't know all the details.

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:38.560
So one, you can
just SSH in, right?

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:39.970
It's a computer.

00:00:39.970 --> 00:00:41.530
SSH works with keys.

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:43.720
So you've got a key
pair, and it works

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:46.870
as-- when you have that token,
you can get into any machine

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:47.950
that the public key's on.

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:52.460
And you can just manage
those all yourself

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:53.877
if you've got a
system to do that.

00:00:53.877 --> 00:00:56.990
But it can get to be a lot
and get quite complicated,

00:00:56.990 --> 00:00:59.320
especially as your
organization gets larger.

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:02.470
And we have a way to delegate
the creation of those keys

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:04.750
to Google's identity
infrastructure.

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:07.480
And that is why I wanted
to bring in Emanuel, who's

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:10.490
one of our colleagues who
focuses on identity and access

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:10.990
management.

00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:12.075
Welcome, Emanuel.

00:01:12.075 --> 00:01:13.450
EMANUEL BURGESS:
Brian, thank you

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:15.830
for that amazing introduction.

00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:17.157
How are you today, Carter?

00:01:17.157 --> 00:01:19.490
CARTER MORGAN: Yeah, I'm doing
well-- a little confused,

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:23.860
but I think Brian can tell
you where my struggles lie.

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:26.650
BRIAN DORSEY: So Emanuel,
what's the high level

00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:28.890
on identity and access
management in Google Cloud?

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:30.890
EMANUEL BURGESS: Brian,
that's a great question.

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:33.550
So let's really go back and
define what is IAM, right?

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:36.610
So IAM stands for Identity
and Access Management.

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:39.970
And so what basically that
means is that you can delegate,

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:43.720
or you can control, who has
access to what resources

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:44.980
in your Google Cloud account.

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:45.730
CARTER MORGAN: OK.

00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:47.620
So you can get your
who has resources,

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:50.170
or who has access
to what resources.

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:51.562
Who's setting that up?

00:01:51.562 --> 00:01:53.020
EMANUEL BURGESS:
Well, so at first,

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:54.530
you need to set up a domain.

00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:57.590
And so there's a person
that's called the super admin.

00:01:57.590 --> 00:02:01.840
So the super admin goes
into the Admin Console,

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:03.800
and they set up your domain.

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:05.560
And so once they
set up your domain,

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:08.320
it automatically creates
an organization for you.

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:11.830
Now, this super admin is
a role who can do anything

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:12.940
within the Admin Portal.

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:15.040
So they can manage users.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:16.960
They can create groups.

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:19.360
They can set up
multifactor authentication.

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:22.168
They can do all of these
things via the Admin Portal.

00:02:22.168 --> 00:02:24.460
BRIAN DORSEY: So we've got
this super admin and domain.

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:26.335
And the thing I'm used
to looking at the most

00:02:26.335 --> 00:02:29.050
is projects.

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:30.222
How do those connect?

00:02:30.222 --> 00:02:31.180
EMANUEL BURGESS: Right.

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:35.270
And so a domain one-to-one
maps with an organization.

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:37.490
And so if we think
of projects, projects

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:39.920
sit under an organization.

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:43.300
And so you can really think
of an organization as the root

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:45.970
node within your GCP account.

00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:48.820
And so with the root node, you
can have things under them,

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:51.130
like projects and folders.

00:02:51.130 --> 00:02:54.880
And then you can assign
resources to projects as well.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:56.150
CARTER MORGAN: Oh.

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:57.850
And then this
hierarchical structure

00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:00.703
is what's used to give access
to different areas, I take it.

00:03:00.703 --> 00:03:01.870
EMANUEL BURGESS: Absolutely.

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:03.285
There you go.

00:03:03.285 --> 00:03:04.660
CARTER MORGAN: So
then what about

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:08.620
if I want a script or something
to access services-- like,

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:11.590
I want a script to access part
of Google Cloud's resources

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:12.100
for me.

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:12.850
What do I do then?

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:16.150
EMANUEL BURGESS: If you want an
outside application or workload

00:03:16.150 --> 00:03:19.330
to access things
within Google Cloud,

00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:21.518
you can use what's
called a service account.

00:03:21.518 --> 00:03:22.810
CARTER MORGAN: And what's that?

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:24.227
EMANUEL BURGESS:
So you can really

00:03:24.227 --> 00:03:26.680
think of a service account
as a special account that's

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:31.210
granted to applications in
order to be able to interact

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:33.160
with APIs within Google Cloud.

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:36.550
And so if we really want
to put this in context,

00:03:36.550 --> 00:03:38.050
let's look at a
user account, right?

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:42.340
So if I'm a user, and I
have access to Google Cloud,

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:48.430
I would go via Chrome to
the Google Cloud website,

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:49.600
and then I would log in.

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:52.180
And so when I put in my
username and password,

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:53.470
I'm authenticated.

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:57.730
After I'm authenticated, I
get to inherit the permissions

00:03:57.730 --> 00:03:59.800
or roles that are
attached to a group

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:01.240
that I'm assigned to, right?

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:05.030
But if I'm an application,
I can't do that.

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:06.970
I can't go and log
in to Google Chrome.

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:09.010
So I would need
a service account

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:11.380
in order to interact
with APIs and services

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:12.545
within Google Cloud.

00:04:12.545 --> 00:04:13.420
BRIAN DORSEY: Got it.

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:14.128
That makes sense.

00:04:14.128 --> 00:04:17.260
So we have these
organization units

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:18.760
that give access to things.

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:20.320
If I'm a user, I
can get in one way.

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:22.150
Maybe I can SSH in.

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:27.610
If I'm programs, I can connect
to other APIs via these service

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:28.420
accounts.

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:33.053
Are there any other ways
that programs get access?

00:04:33.053 --> 00:04:33.970
EMANUEL BURGESS: Yeah.

00:04:33.970 --> 00:04:35.680
So you have service accounts.

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:38.680
And then you also have
service account keys, right?

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:42.040
So back in the day, in
the way, way distant past,

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:45.640
right, we gave outside
applications access

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:49.120
to workloads or resources
in Google Cloud using

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:50.590
service account keys, right?

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:53.780
But service account
keys are problematic.

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:57.070
One of the things that make
service account keys really not

00:04:57.070 --> 00:05:00.730
a good choice if you're trying
to really securely build

00:05:00.730 --> 00:05:05.890
resources in Google Cloud is
because they never end, right?

00:05:05.890 --> 00:05:07.160
They live on forever.

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:09.550
And so service account
keys, if they're leaked,

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:12.080
can cause a really major issue.

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:14.950
And so whoever has access to
your service account keys,

00:05:14.950 --> 00:05:17.200
in theory, can impersonate
a service account

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:20.650
and get access to all the
APIs and all the features that

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:23.020
are attached to that
service account.

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:25.990
And then in addition to
that, this causes a secrets

00:05:25.990 --> 00:05:27.280
management issue, right?

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:31.220
So how do we manage these keys
that are just floating around?

00:05:31.220 --> 00:05:35.200
So now, you have to come up with
a secrets management strategy

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:38.320
in order to
safeguard and provide

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:41.380
some type of peace of mind
around these service account

00:05:41.380 --> 00:05:43.187
keys.

00:05:43.187 --> 00:05:45.270
CARTER MORGAN: I never
actually thought about some

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:47.670
of these downsides of
service account keys.

00:05:47.670 --> 00:05:50.547
And I like just
saying, these things

00:05:50.547 --> 00:05:51.630
from the way distant past.

00:05:51.630 --> 00:05:53.805
I love that.

00:05:53.805 --> 00:05:55.290
What you just talked
about, though,

00:05:55.290 --> 00:05:57.990
made me think of something.

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:01.350
It seems like service accounts
are used for resources

00:06:01.350 --> 00:06:03.320
within Google Cloud.

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:06.830
What if I have some service
outside of Google Cloud

00:06:06.830 --> 00:06:09.890
that I want to
authenticate into?

00:06:09.890 --> 00:06:12.320
Is there any way to
let Google manage that?

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:13.487
EMANUEL BURGESS: Well, yeah.

00:06:13.487 --> 00:06:14.420
So we can do that.

00:06:14.420 --> 00:06:16.340
So let's say I'm using
Terraform, right?

00:06:16.340 --> 00:06:18.590
So I want to build
infrastructure

00:06:18.590 --> 00:06:21.290
within Google Cloud using a
tool called Terraform, right?

00:06:21.290 --> 00:06:22.470
So I can do that.

00:06:22.470 --> 00:06:26.150
So long ago, in
order for me to do

00:06:26.150 --> 00:06:29.900
that, in order for the
outside application Terraform

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:32.840
to interact with the resources
in my Google Cloud account,

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:35.300
I had to give it service
account keys, right?

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:36.830
And so I would set up Terraform.

00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:38.360
I would create a provider block.

00:06:38.360 --> 00:06:40.550
I would pass it my
service account keys.

00:06:40.550 --> 00:06:44.060
And the service account keys
would impersonate a service

00:06:44.060 --> 00:06:45.930
account within
Google Cloud, right?

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:46.700
So that worked.

00:06:46.700 --> 00:06:49.980
But now, I can
build my resources,

00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:52.050
but now, I have to
manage that specific key.

00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:55.310
So now, we can use what's
called short-lived credentials,

00:06:55.310 --> 00:06:58.530
and I don't have to worry about
managing these service account

00:06:58.530 --> 00:06:59.030
keys.

00:06:59.030 --> 00:07:02.480
So now, if I want to
build infrastructure

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:05.600
in Google Cloud
using Terraform, I

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:08.210
can use short-lived
credentials in order

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:11.245
to authenticate Terraform
with Google Cloud.

00:07:11.245 --> 00:07:12.620
CARTER MORGAN: I
love how excited

00:07:12.620 --> 00:07:14.720
you were to tell me more about
these short-lived tokens.

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:15.050
EMANUEL BURGESS: I love it.

00:07:15.050 --> 00:07:15.380
I love it.

00:07:15.380 --> 00:07:17.130
CARTER MORGAN: Because
they are that cool.

00:07:17.130 --> 00:07:18.350
They really are.

00:07:18.350 --> 00:07:19.320
So thank you.

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:19.820
All right.

00:07:19.820 --> 00:07:23.100
So but now, to the
question before--

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:29.420
is it possible to get this
kind of security or access

00:07:29.420 --> 00:07:30.623
to external workflows?

00:07:30.623 --> 00:07:31.790
EMANUEL BURGESS: Well, yeah.

00:07:31.790 --> 00:07:34.700
So we also have a feature
that's called workload identity

00:07:34.700 --> 00:07:35.570
federation.

00:07:35.570 --> 00:07:38.240
And so if you have
workloads that are external

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:42.680
or outside of Google Cloud
that have their own identity

00:07:42.680 --> 00:07:47.160
provider, you can authenticate
using these short-lived tokens.

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:50.000
And so what happens is you
have an application who

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:53.010
gets credentials from
its identity provider,

00:07:53.010 --> 00:07:55.340
and then those
credentials are passed

00:07:55.340 --> 00:07:57.830
to Google's token service.

00:07:57.830 --> 00:08:01.520
And Google token service
takes those credentials,

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:04.460
creates an ephemeral
short-lived token

00:08:04.460 --> 00:08:07.400
that it passes to the
external application.

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:11.930
And now the external application
can access and impersonate

00:08:11.930 --> 00:08:14.855
a service account
within Google Cloud.

00:08:14.855 --> 00:08:15.730
BRIAN DORSEY: Got it.

00:08:15.730 --> 00:08:16.230
OK.

00:08:16.230 --> 00:08:19.460
So we've got a lot of different
kinds of access here, right?

00:08:19.460 --> 00:08:22.510
So we've got users, service
accounts, short-lived tokens,

00:08:22.510 --> 00:08:25.351
workload stuff.

00:08:25.351 --> 00:08:28.300
How do I decide
which ones of these

00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:29.988
to use in a given situation?

00:08:29.988 --> 00:08:32.030
EMANUEL BURGESS: I mean,
that's a great question.

00:08:32.030 --> 00:08:33.072
So it all depends, right?

00:08:33.072 --> 00:08:38.590
So if I'm a user, and I just
have a outside application that

00:08:38.590 --> 00:08:40.870
doesn't have an identity
provider-- let's

00:08:40.870 --> 00:08:43.780
say, once again, I'm
using Terraform, right?

00:08:43.780 --> 00:08:45.580
I can use short-lived
credentials

00:08:45.580 --> 00:08:48.670
to authenticate that
outside application

00:08:48.670 --> 00:08:51.310
and impersonate a service
account within Google Cloud.

00:08:51.310 --> 00:08:55.000
If I have a workload that has
an identity provider-- like,

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:58.540
let's say I'm using AWS
for my identity provider,

00:08:58.540 --> 00:09:01.180
or I'm using on-premise
Active Directory,

00:09:01.180 --> 00:09:02.980
or I'm using Azure
Active Directory,

00:09:02.980 --> 00:09:04.870
or let's even say
I'm using Okta.

00:09:04.870 --> 00:09:08.320
In those cases, it would be
best to use workload identity

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:10.360
federation so you
can dynamically

00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:13.840
generate keys and not have to
worry about manually generating

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:19.430
keys from gcloud command line
or one of the other methods.

00:09:19.430 --> 00:09:22.338
BRIAN DORSEY: And then
all of this taps into IAM.

00:09:22.338 --> 00:09:23.380
Actually, that's awesome.

00:09:23.380 --> 00:09:25.120
So you've got a consistent
way to look at it.

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:25.870
That's super cool.

00:09:25.870 --> 00:09:28.540
So if somebody wants
to dig in more on this,

00:09:28.540 --> 00:09:30.040
where should they start?

00:09:30.040 --> 00:09:33.760
EMANUEL BURGESS: In order to
get more information about IAM,

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:37.630
security best practices,
workload identity federation,

00:09:37.630 --> 00:09:39.700
short-lived credentials,
you would go

00:09:39.700 --> 00:09:45.310
to cloud.google.com/IAM/docs.

00:09:45.310 --> 00:09:47.005
CARTER MORGAN: Yeah.

00:09:47.005 --> 00:09:48.880
And thank you so much,
Emanuel, for coming in

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:52.120
because I had never heard
of short-lived tokens.

00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:55.510
I'd never heard of workload
identity federation.

00:09:55.510 --> 00:09:57.970
And I never really thought
about some of the downsides

00:09:57.970 --> 00:10:01.720
to even service accounts and
the long-lived static keys.

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:07.000
So to recap, would you say using
IAM is a best practice, maybe,

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:07.993
going forward?

00:10:07.993 --> 00:10:09.160
EMANUEL BURGESS: Absolutely.

00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:13.690
I think, if you're working
in any large organization,

00:10:13.690 --> 00:10:16.000
setting up IAM
using organizations

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:17.630
is really the best way to go.

00:10:17.630 --> 00:10:20.260
Now, if you wanted to just
play around with Google Cloud,

00:10:20.260 --> 00:10:22.510
you could sign up with
just your Gmail account.

00:10:22.510 --> 00:10:25.000
But if you did that, that
wouldn't give you access

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:27.850
to all the benefits of
organizations, right?

00:10:27.850 --> 00:10:29.950
So if you're at a
large enterprise,

00:10:29.950 --> 00:10:35.020
organizations, to invoke IAM
on your Google Cloud account

00:10:35.020 --> 00:10:36.268
is the best way to go.

00:10:36.268 --> 00:10:37.060
CARTER MORGAN: Wow.

00:10:37.060 --> 00:10:38.140
So that's about all.

00:10:38.140 --> 00:10:40.810
I learned so much today
about workload federation,

00:10:40.810 --> 00:10:42.820
short-lived tokens, and more.

00:10:42.820 --> 00:10:44.680
Brian, Thanks for
bringing Emanuel on.

00:10:44.680 --> 00:10:45.898
I got to give you props.

00:10:45.898 --> 00:10:47.440
BRIAN DORSEY: No,
thank you, Emanuel.

00:10:47.440 --> 00:10:49.623
This was great.

00:10:49.623 --> 00:10:51.790
EMANUEL BURGESS: I appreciate
you, Brian and Carter.

00:10:51.790 --> 00:10:53.368
Thank you guys for
the opportunity.

00:10:53.368 --> 00:10:54.160
CARTER MORGAN: Yes.

00:10:54.160 --> 00:10:56.710
If you're listening at home,
leave a comment with something

00:10:56.710 --> 00:10:57.220
you learned.

00:10:57.220 --> 00:11:00.053
